{{also|CRAP|crăp}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɹæp/}}
* {{rhymes|en|æp|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-crap.ogg|a=US}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|crappe}}, also in plural: {{m|enm|crappys}}, {{m|enm|craps||chaff; buckwheat}}, from {{der|en|frm|crape}}, from {{der|en|fro|crappe}}, {{m|fro|crapin||chaff}} (compare {{cog|ML.|crappa|g=p}}, also {{m|la|crapinum}}), from {{der|en|odt|krappen||to cut off, pluck off}} (whence {{cog|dum|crappe}}, {{m|dum|crap||a chop, cutlet}}, whence {{cog|nl|krip||a steak}}). Related to {{m|en|crop}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[husk]] of [[grain]]; [[chaff]].
# {{lb|en|slang|mildly|vulgar|uncountable}} Something worthless or of poor quality; junk.
#: {{ux|en|The long-running game show went from offering good prizes to '''crap''' in no time.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|mildly|vulgar|uncountable}} Nonsense; something untrue.
#: {{ux|en|The college student boasted of completing a 10,000-word essay on Shakespeare, but that claim was utter '''crap'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|mildly|vulgar}} [[faeces|Faeces]]/[[feces]].
#: {{ux|en|I stepped in some dog '''crap''' that was on the sidewalk.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|mildly|vulgar|countable}} An act of [[defecation]].
#: {{ux|en|I have to take a '''crap'''.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The vulgarity of ''crap'' is a contentious topic, with many considering it to be a swear word while others do not.  Nevertheless, it is generally considered to be impolite and is typically avoided in formal speech and writing.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|faeces}} {{l|en|poop}}, {{l|en|poo}}, {{l|en|dump}}, {{l|en|shit}}. Note: often used as a less vulgar synonym for, or minced form of, {{l|en|shit}} in all its senses.

